<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munger</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monghyr, Mudgagiri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Munger, formerly Monghyr, is a city on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was an important centre of the Pala Empire, where royal grants were issued from a victory camp, and near it the Pratihara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a> defeated the Pala king Dharmapala in the early ninth century. In the 1760s the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> made it his capital, and it was later known for its fort and gun-making.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munger.jpg" alt="Munger" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rkrjmp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_of_Munger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Munger lies on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, about 180 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> and 480 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. It forms a twin city with Jamalpur and is the headquarters of Munger district and division. Historically it sat at a strategic bottleneck on the main route into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from the west: steep hills blocked travel to the south, and the many tributaries of the Ganges were the obstacle to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town is said to have been founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> in the fourth century CE. In the classical period it was called Guptagadh or Guptagarh, and an inscription on a rock at the kasta-harani ghat, near the north-western side of the present fort, names it as founded by Chandragupta Vikramaditya. In early medieval times it was Mudgagiri, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> mudga, the mung bean, and giri, hill. It was a royal residence of the Pala Empire, and a significant battle between the Palas and the Pratiharas was fought there as part of a Pratihara invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>It was later mentioned as the residence of the Pala ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a>. In the twelfth century it seems to have been under Sena rule, and in April 1146 the Gahadavala ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govindachandra-of-the-gahadavala-dynasty">Govindachandra</a> advanced as far as Mudgagiri and bathed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, probably on an unsuccessful expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>At the end of the twelfth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a> conquered Munger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. The region was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> until 1330, when Muhammad Tughlaq annexed it, and from 1397 it belonged to the Jaunpur Sultanate until Sikander Lodi overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> before 1499. In 1521 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nusrat Shah</a> annexed a few of its forts, and it later fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>. From 1545 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty">Karrani dynasty</a> held it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s invasion of Bengal.</p><p>In 1763 the Nawab of Bengal Mir Qasim made Munger his capital and built an arsenal and several palaces. The British, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in the 1760s, made it a municipality in 1864 and set up the railway workshops at Jamalpur in 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fort covers about 222 acres and holds the tomb of the Muslim saint Shah Mushk Nafā, who died in 1497. Munger is also known for the Bihar School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a>, founded in 1964, and for the Sitacharan temple on a boulder in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, a centre of the Chhath festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Munger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vatsaraja defeated the Pala king Dharmapala but was routed by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, and Nagabhata II, after taking Kannauj and defeating Dharmapala near <b>Monghyr</b>, was in turn defeated by Govinda III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, yet managed to place Chakrayudha on the throne of Kannauj after both rivals withdrew; around 800 the Rashtrakuta Govinda III marched north and received the submission of both Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and Nagabhata II defeated Dharmapala near <b>Monghyr</b> and took Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
